#THE TASK
#Implement a program that prompts the user for the name of a variable in camel case and outputs the corresponding name in snake case. Assume that the user’s input will indeed be in camel case
#Camel Case = when words/variables comprise multiple words, whereby the first letter of the first word is lowercase but the first letter of each subsequent word is uppercase (e.g. firstName)
#Snake Case = words/variables are instead separated by underscores (_), with all letters in lowercase (e.g. first_name)

#THOUGHTS...
#Given the input: preferedFirstName -> we want: prefered_first_name as output
#The central task is: inspect an input one letter at a time and build the output progressively
#Lowercase characters remain unchanged
#Uppercase characters mark the beginning of a new word (e.g.First)
#The uppercase character must become lowercase + a _ must be inserted before it (e.g. _first)

#The solution architecture is something like:

#for each character:
    #if the character is lowercase:
        #output it unchanged
    #else:
        #output "_" followed by its lowercase version

#if character is uppercase:
    #add "_" and its lowercase version
#else:
    #add the character unchanged

#SOLUTION

#Create a new variable asking the user for the CameCase input (we assume the user inputs in CamelCase)
camel_case = input("CamelCase: ")

#Create an empty string variable that will hold the converted user input
snake_case = ""

#We take one character at a time from the camelcase via a for loop (character is our made up name for the variable that holds the temporary character/string item in the for loop)
#Python knows it is examining characters because the variable camel_case always takes a string as input 
#We are saying for each character inside the string stored in camel_case run the indented code block 
for character in camel_case:
    if character.isupper(): #Implementing a conditional if/else
        snake_case = snake_case + "_" + character.lower() #Convert character to lowercase, place _ before, and add to new string
    else:
        snake_case = snake_case + character #If it is not uppercase transfer it unchanged to the new string

#character.lower is a string method/operation (character is the string we are operating on and lower is the method name - () calls/executes the method)

#Then we display the new string - we literally print snake_case: and then add the content of the variable snake_case that was changed/altered  
print("snake_case:", snake_case) #This works because the variable snake_case is a string

#We could also print it with an f string
# print(f"snake_case: {snake_case}")
#The f creates a formatted string and tels python to first evaluate and then insert the value of anything in the curly braces - without the f python treats everything as normal text/string and prints the braces literally "{snake_case}"





